<topic xml:id="rt-io-cpu-tuning-change-io-priority" xml:lang="en"
       role="task"
       xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.1"
       xmlns:its="http://www.w3.org/2005/11/its"
       xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
       xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">
    <info>
        <meta name="maintainer" content="daria.vladykina@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
        <title>How to manage process I/O priorities with <command>ionice</command>?</title>
        <abstract>
            <para>
                Inspect and adjust process I/O scheduling classes and priority levels using the
                <command>ionice</command> utility.
            </para>
        </abstract>
    </info>
    <procedure xml:id="pro-change-io-priority">
        <title>Setting I/O priorities with <command>ionice</command></title>
        <para>
            To view or modify process I/O scheduling attributes, perform the following steps.
        </para>
        <step>
            <para>
                To set the I/O scheduling class of the current shell to <literal>idle</literal>, run:
            </para>
<screen>&prompt.user;<command>ionice -c3 -p$$</command></screen>
        </step>
        <step>
            <para>
                To print the I/O scheduling class and priority of the current shell,
                run <command>ionice</command> without additional parameters:
            </para>
<screen>&prompt.user;<command>ionice</command></screen>
        </step>
        <step>
            <para>
                To set the I/O scheduling class of the process with the process ID
                <literal>42</literal> to real-time and its priority level to
                <literal>2</literal>, run:
            </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>ionice -c1 -p42 -n2</command></screen>
        </step>
        <step>
            <para>
                To start the Bash shell in the <literal>idle</literal> class, run:
            </para>
<screen>&prompt.user;<command>ionice -c3 /bin/bash</command></screen>
        </step>
    </procedure>
</topic>
